<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<Package
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
  xmlns:mp="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/2014/phone/manifest"
  xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
  xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities"
  IgnorableNamespaces="uap rescap">

  <!--
    TODO (blocked by OQ-6, issue #39): three values here and one below come from
    Partner Center and are the only thing standing between this manifest and a
    submittable package. Reserve the app name in Partner Center, then copy from
    Product identity:

      Name       <- "Package/Identity/Name"       e.g. 12345TonyGoodhew.WinZ3805A
      Publisher  <- "Package/Identity/Publisher"  e.g. CN=A1B2C3D4-...-E5F6
      (below)    <- "Package/Properties/PublisherDisplayName"

    Copy them verbatim. The publisher is a distinguished name that must match the
    certificate the Store signs with exactly - retyping it from memory is the
    usual way a first submission fails.

    Identity/@Name is effectively permanent: changing it later creates a new app
    with a new listing and no upgrade path for existing users. Properties
    /DisplayName below is deliberately decoupled from it (§6.3) and can be
    changed at any time without consequence.

    Until then these are the SIDELOAD identity, not placeholders in the sense of
    being arbitrary. Publisher was "CN=AppPublisher" until the first public
    release (30 Aug 2026), and it had to stop being that before anyone installed:
    the publisher string is what the trust prompt shows, and asking a person to
    trust a certificate issued to "AppPublisher" is asking them to make a
    security decision on no information. It now matches PublisherDisplayName
    below, so the prompt names the entity the package already claims.

    IT ALSO COULD NOT BE CHANGED LATER. Name and Publisher together form the
    package family name; changing either means the next version installs
    ALONGSIDE the old one rather than upgrading it. That is why this was settled
    before the first release rather than after.

    Version is the release version and must equal the tag that builds it -
    .github/workflows/release.yml fails the release if they disagree, rather
    than rewriting one to match the other, so this file stays the one place a
    version is decided.

    These still would be rejected at submission: Partner Center issues its own
    Name and Publisher and they must match the account exactly.
  -->
  <Identity
    Name="WinZ3805A"
    Publisher="CN=The Schanzuer Group LLC"
    Version="1.0.1.0" />

  <mp:PhoneIdentity PhoneProductId="DB5D1AF4-65FD-41CA-91F9-D83D5ACEB3B0" PhonePublisherId="00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"/>

  <Properties>
    <!-- TODO (OQ-8): decide whether this survives Store certification as-is, or
         becomes descriptive (e.g. "GPSDO Monitor for Z3805A"). Read at runtime
         via Package.Current.DisplayName; never hard-coded in XAML or tests. -->
    <DisplayName>WinZ3805A</DisplayName>
    <!--
      The publisher as a person reads it. This is display only, unlike the
      Publisher distinguished name in Identity above, which is bound to the
      signing certificate and to package identity (§6.3) and is NOT changed here.

      Still subject to OQ-6 / #39 at Store submission: Partner Center issues its
      own publisher display name and it must match the account, so this may be
      overwritten by the value they give. It is correct for the sideloaded build,
      which is the only one that exists.
    -->
    <PublisherDisplayName>The Schanzuer Group LLC</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Logo>Assets\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
  </Properties>

  <Dependencies>
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Universal" MinVersion="10.0.17763.0" MaxVersionTested="10.0.26226.0" />
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Desktop" MinVersion="10.0.17763.0" MaxVersionTested="10.0.26226.0" />
  </Dependencies>

  <Resources>
    <Resource Language="x-generate"/>
  </Resources>

  <!--
    The privacy policy URL Store policy requires is NOT declared here - the
    manifest has no element for it. It is entered in Partner Center against the
    listing. The policy itself is docs/privacy.md, published by GitHub Pages;
    see docs/store-listing.md for the URL and the rest of the submission copy.
  -->

  <Applications>
    <Application Id="App"
      Executable="$targetnametoken$.exe"
      EntryPoint="$targetentrypoint$">
      <!--
        Every logo here names the unqualified file. MSIX resolves the
        .scale-100 through .scale-400 and .targetsize-* variants beside it from
        the resource index, so listing one is listing all of them; naming a
        qualified file instead pins the app to a single display scaling.

        The four tile sizes are all supplied. Windows falls back to scaling the
        150x150 down when the small tile is absent, and a segmented ring
        resampled to 71 px loses its gaps - see build/New-AppAssets.ps1, which
        redraws the mark at each size rather than resampling one.
      -->
      <uap:VisualElements
        DisplayName="WinZ3805A"
        Description="Monitor and control application for SCPI GPS-disciplined oscillators."
        BackgroundColor="transparent"
        Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
        Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png">
        <uap:DefaultTile
          Square71x71Logo="Assets\Square71x71Logo.png"
          Square310x310Logo="Assets\Square310x310Logo.png"
          Wide310x150Logo="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.png" />
        <uap:SplashScreen Image="Assets\SplashScreen.png" />
      </uap:VisualElements>

      <!--
        NO COM SERVER AND NO TOAST ACTIVATOR. Both were added on 28 Aug 2026 to satisfy
        AppNotificationManager, whose Register() then failed with 0x80070490 on every machine
        anyway, and both were removed on 29 Aug 2026 with that API (issue 288).

        Notifications now go through ToastNotificationManager, which needs no declaration here at
        all - only package identity. They are deliverable BECAUSE §8 forbids a notification carrying
        buttons or arguments: activation is the only thing an activator provides, and nothing here
        can be activated. Do not add one back without a reason that survives that rule.
      -->
    </Application>
  </Applications>

  <!--
    §6.3: runFullTrust only. It is what permits System.IO.Ports, which opens
    \\.\COMn through Win32. It is a restricted capability and needs justification
    at submission:
      "Desktop application requiring Win32 serial port access to communicate with
       user-attached RS-232 laboratory instruments."

    Do NOT declare the serialcommunication DeviceCapability - that is for the UWP
    Windows.Devices.SerialCommunication API, which this app does not use.
    Unnecessary capabilities add certification friction. The template's
    systemAIModels capability was removed for the same reason.
  -->
  <Capabilities>
    <rescap:Capability Name="runFullTrust" />
  </Capabilities>
</Package>
